in DreamerV3 and it didn't work well\"?\n- Does the factorial ablation methodology itself constitute a contribution, or is it standard practice?\n- Are there important missing references (e.g., TD-MPC, IRIS, other world-model RL with temporal abstraction; ensemble-based uncertainty in MBRL like PETS; model-predictive approaches)?\n- Does the paper adequately situate itself relative to model exploitation literature (Janner et al. 2019 already identify model exploitation as a key failure mode)?\n- What is the takeaway for the community? Is it actionable?\n- The paper's own Table 18 downgrades most claims — does this self-assessment undermine the contribution?\n- Is restricting to DMC Suite (relatively simple continuous control) and DreamerV3 (one specific architecture) sufficient to support the paper's broad claims about \"temporal abstraction in world-model RL\"?\n","uuid":"af353b37-643e-4561-b445-9506a4b508ae","session_id":"aa49a6eb-652d-4e6b-ad9c-09d11a6223fd"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **novelty**, **significance**, and **related work**. Review standard: top ML venue, ~25% acceptance rate, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"When Does Temporal Abstraction Break World Models? An Empirical Investigation of DreamerV3\"\n\nThe paper investigates when and why variable-rate imagination fails in DreamerV3. Through a factorial ablation across 4 DMC environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop) with 3 seeds per condition, they find:\n- Pathological value overestimation (nve > 1.5×) and collapse occur ONLY in Walker Walk (2/3 seeds exceeding threshold, 1/3 collapsing)\n- Cheetah, Cartpole, Hopper show uniform degradation without pathological overestimation (0/9 seeds exceeding 1.5×)\n- The divergence is traced to environment-specific RSSM prediction fidelity (Walker obs_gap=2.04× vs Cheetah 1.35×)\n- A dt=1 imagination mitigation prevents collapse but recovers only 73% baseline (IQM, range 60-95%)\n\nKey contributions claimed:\n1. Factorial ablation framework for diagnosing temporal abstraction failures\n2. Normalized value/eval (nve) monitoring protocol (threshold >1.5× at ≥35K steps)\n3. Evidence that failure modes are architecture×environment interactions\n4. Characterization of dt=1 imagination as environment-conditional partial mitigation\n\n## Related Work Covered in Paper\n\nThe paper cites:\n- Options framework: Sutton et al. 1999, Precup 2000\n- Option-critic: Bacon et al. 2017\n- Deliberation cost: Harb et al. 2018\n- Action repetition: Lakshminarayanan et al. 2017, FiGAR (Sharma et al. 2017)\n- Dreamer family: Hafner et al. 2020, 2021, 2023\n- Value overestimation in MBRL: Janner et al. 2019 (MBPO), Lu et al. 2018 (non-delusional Q-learning)\n- Primacy bias: Nikishin et al. 2022\n\n## Key Evidence and Findings\n\n**Cross-environment:** The delusional critic mechanism is Walker-specific. 0/9 non-Walker seeds exceed nve>1.5×. dt_emb causal effect is negligible in Cheetah/Cartpole/Hopper (~3-5% gap between trained and untrained).\n\n**Diagnostic metric (nve):** Classification accuracy 67% (4/6) in Walker. Untested outside environments with pathological dynamics. The 1.5× threshold was calibrated on Walker baseline v/e.\n\n**RSSM fidelity analysis:** obs_gap explains cross-env divergence post-hoc but has weak predictive power (r≈0.45 correlation with collapse).\n\n**Mitigation:** dt=1 imagination prevents collapse but 73% IQM recovery with ±20pp CI.\n\n**Supplementary:** Actor entropy (no independent predictive power), cosine similarity (inconclusive), uncertainty-gated truncation (infeasible at 27% baseline).\n\n**Claim viability self-assessment (Table 18):**\n- C1 (degradation): Strong → Strong\n- C2 (nve diagnostic): Moderate-strong → Weak\n- C3 (value suppression): Strong → Moderate\n- C4 (dt amplification): Moderate → Moderate\n- C5 (horizon/subsample): Moderate → Moderate-strong\n- C6 (mitigation): Moderate-strong → Weak-moderate\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, focus on novelty, significance, positioning, related work)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification, 1-2 sentences on confidence)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5, where 5 = very confident)\n\nBe rigorous. Pay special attention to:\n- Is the \"negative finding\" (non-replication across environments) novel or expected?\n- Is the contribution primarily \"we tried temporal abstraction in DreamerV3 and it didn't work well\"?\n- Does the factorial ablation methodology itself constitute a contribution, or is it standard practice?\n- Are there important missing references (e.g., TD-MPC, IRIS, other world-model RL with temporal abstraction; ensemble-based uncertainty in MBRL like PETS; model-predictive approaches)?\n- Does the paper adequately situate itself relative to model exploitation literature (Janner et al. 2019 already identify model exploitation as a key failure mode)?\n- What is the takeaway for the community? Is it actionable?\n- The paper's own Table 18 downgrades most claims — does this self-assessment undermine the contribution?\n- Is restricting to DMC Suite (relatively simple continuous control) and DreamerV3 (one specific architecture) sufficient to support the paper's broad claims about \"temporal abstraction in world-model RL\"?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ULYQdJC4FEnAQtNq7j8RxB","session_id":"aa49a6eb-652d-4e6b-ad9c-09d11a6223fd","uuid":"32f07f32-f1ad-42e6-87f1-f293d7cde5c4","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:03:04.413Z"}
