{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q4djeSo8D6SqT7ATfkYBSH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{caption}\n13\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n14\t\\usepackage{makecell}\n15\t\n16\t\\title{When Does Temporal Abstraction Break World Models?\\\\ An Empirical Investigation of DreamerV3}\n17\t\n18\t\\author{\n19\tAnonymous Authors\n20\t}\n21\t\\date{}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\tTemporal abstraction---imagining at variable time scales---is a natural extension for world-model reinforcement learning, yet practical implementations consistently underperform fixed-rate baselines. We present a controlled empirical investigation asking \\emph{when and why} variable-rate imagination fails in DreamerV3. Through a factorial ablation framework across four DeepMind Control environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop) with 3 seeds per condition, we find that the failure mode is \\emph{not} universal: pathological value overestimation (normalized value/eval ratio $>$1.5$\\times$ baseline) and catastrophic collapse occur only in Walker Walk (2/3 seeds exceeding the threshold, 1/3 collapsing), while Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, and Hopper Hop show uniform performance degradation without pathological overestimation (0/9 seeds exceeding 1.5$\\times$). We trace this divergence to environment-specific RSSM prediction fidelity: Walker's observation prediction gap at $\\Delta t{=}2$ is 2.04$\\times$ (vs.\\ 1.35$\\times$ for Cheetah), creating sufficient model error for trained $\\Delta t$-embeddings to amplify into a delusional critic feedback loop. Our diagnostic methodology---factorial ablation combined with a normalized value/eval (nve) monitoring protocol---identifies the interaction between architecture choice and environment dynamics as the primary determinant of failure mode, rather than any single architectural component. A simple mitigation (restricting imagination to $\\Delta t{=}1$ during multi-rate training) prevents collapse across all seeds but recovers only 73\\% of baseline performance (IQM, range 60--95\\%), limiting its practical value. These findings suggest that temporal abstraction failures in world-model RL resist universal diagnosis: the failure mode is a product of architecture$\\times$environment interaction.\n29\t\\end{abstract}\n30\t\n31\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n32\t\\section{Introduction}\n33\t\\label{sec:intro}\n34\t\n35\tTemporal abstraction---the ability to reason and act at multiple time scales---is a long-sought capability in reinforcement learning~\\citep{sutton1999between,precup2000temporal}. World-model RL architectures such as DreamerV3~\\citep{hafner2023mastering} offer a natural testbed: by modifying the temporal step size $\\Delta t$ of imagination rollouts, an agent can plan at variable rates without altering environment interaction. This \\emph{variable-rate imagination} promises more efficient credit assignment and longer effective planning horizons.\n36\t\n37\tYet in practice, all our attempts at variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 degrade performance relative to the $\\Delta t{=}1$ baseline. Fixed $\\Delta t{=}2$ imagination collapses catastrophically; $\\Delta t{=}4$ produces severe degradation (33\\% of baseline); learned gating mechanisms fail similarly. The question is not \\emph{whether} it fails, but \\emph{when and why}---and whether the failure mechanism generalizes.\n38\t\n39\tWe conduct a systematic factorial investigation across four DeepMind Control Suite environments, isolating the roles of $\\Delta t$-embedding training, temporal subsampling, and imagination horizon. Our central finding is negative in a specific sense: the delusional critic mechanism---where trained $\\Delta t$-embeddings amplify RSSM prediction errors into pathological value overestimation---is \\emph{environment-specific}. It manifests clearly in Walker Walk (where RSSM fidelity at $\\Delta t{>}1$ is poor) but does not replicate in Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, or Hopper Hop (where RSSM predictions at $\\Delta t{=}2$ remain adequate). This non-replication is itself a finding: there is no universal failure mode for temporal abstraction in world-model RL. The failure is a product of the interaction between model architecture and environment dynamics.\n40\t\n41\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n42\t\\begin{enumerate}\n43\t    \\item A \\textbf{factorial ablation framework} for diagnosing temporal abstraction failures, isolating $\\Delta t$-embedding, subsampling, and horizon effects through controlled comparisons.\n44\t    \\item A \\textbf{normalized value/eval (nve) monitoring protocol} that flags pathological overestimation when the experiment's value/eval ratio exceeds 1.5$\\times$ the baseline ratio at the same training step (applicable after 35K steps when baseline calibration stabilizes).\n45\t    \\item Evidence that \\textbf{temporal abstraction failure modes are architecture$\\times$environment interactions}: the delusional critic mechanism triggers only when RSSM fidelity gap at $\\Delta t{>}1$ is sufficiently large (Walker: obs\\_gap = 2.04$\\times$) and does not trigger when the gap is moderate (Cheetah: 1.35$\\times$, Cartpole: 1.45$\\times$, Hopper: 1.55$\\times$).\n46\t    \\item A characterization of \\textbf{$\\Delta t{=}1$ imagination restriction} as an environment-conditional partial mitigation: it prevents collapse but achieves only 73\\% baseline recovery (IQM across 3 seeds) with high cross-seed variance.\n47\t\\end{enumerate}\n48\t\n49\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n50\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n51\t\\label{sec:background}\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in RL.}\n54\tThe options framework~\\citep{sutton1999between} and its extensions~\\citep{bacon2017option,harb2018when} formalize multi-timescale decision making in model-free settings. Action repetition~\\citep{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic} and FiGAR~\\citep{sharma2017learning} offer simpler temporal abstractions. These approaches modify the \\emph{acting} timescale; we instead modify the \\emph{imagination} timescale within a learned world model.\n55\t\n56\t\\paragraph{World-model RL.}\n57\tThe Dreamer family~\\citep{hafner2020dream,hafner2021mastering,hafner2023mastering} learns latent dynamics models (RSSM) and trains policies entirely in imagination. DreamerV3~\\citep{hafner2023mastering} achieves strong performance across diverse domains using categorical latent states and symlog normalization. We use DreamerV3 as our base architecture because its RSSM is representative of current latent-dynamics world models.\n58\t\n59\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation in model-based RL.}\n60\tModel exploitation and value overestimation are known failure modes in MBRL~\\citep{janner2019trust,lu2018non}. Our work identifies a \\emph{specific} mechanism through which temporal abstraction triggers overestimation: trained $\\Delta t$-embeddings enable precise temporal conditioning in imagination, but RSSM prediction quality at $\\Delta t{>}1$ is insufficient to support this precision, creating systematic optimistic bias.\n61\t\n62\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n63\t\\section{Methodology}\n64\t\\label{sec:method}\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Base Architecture}\n67\t\n68\tWe build on DreamerV3~\\citep{hafner2023mastering} with its standard RSSM world model. To enable variable-rate imagination, we introduce a $\\Delta t$-embedding: a learned vector that conditions the RSSM transition model on the temporal step size. During imagination, the agent can rollout trajectories at $\\Delta t \\in \\{1, 2\\}$, allowing longer effective planning horizons.\n69\t\n70\t\\subsection{Factorial Experiment Design}\n71\t\n72\tWe design a factorial ablation to isolate three factors:\n73\t\n74\t\\begin{itemize}\n75\t    \\item \\textbf{$\\Delta t$-embedding}: \\emph{trained} (learns to differentiate $\\Delta t$ values), \\emph{untrained} (random fixed embedding), or \\emph{absent} ($\\texttt{dt\\_embed\\_dim}{=}0$).\n76\t    \\item \\textbf{Temporal subsampling}: \\emph{ON} (training data includes $\\Delta t{=}2$ transitions alongside $\\Delta t{=}1$) or \\emph{OFF} (only $\\Delta t{=}1$ transitions in training).\n77\t    \\item \\textbf{Imagination horizon}: $H \\in \\{8, 15\\}$ steps.\n78\t\\end{itemize}\n79\t\n80\tAll experiments use the same DreamerV3 hyperparameters except the ablated factors. We evaluate across four DMC environments: Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, and Hopper Hop, with 3 random seeds per configuration.\n81\t\n82\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Normalized Value/Eval ratio (nve).}\n85\tWe define the nve at training step $t$ as:\n86\t\\begin{equation}\n87\t    \\text{nve}(t) = \\frac{\\text{v/e}_{\\text{exp}}(t)}{\\text{v/e}_{\\text{baseline}}(t)}\n88\t\\end{equation}\n89\twhere $\\text{v/e}(t)$ is the ratio of the critic's mean value prediction to the mean evaluation return at step $t$. An nve $> 1.5$ at $t \\geq 35$K (after baseline v/e calibration stabilizes) indicates pathological overestimation. We use 35K as the reliability threshold because baseline v/e at 25K is only $\\sim$0.087, making ratios unstable.\n90\t\n91\t\\paragraph{RSSM fidelity gap.}\n92\tFor each model, we measure one-step ($\\Delta t{=}1$) and two-step ($\\Delta t{=}2$) prediction errors (observation MSE, reward MAE) and compute the gap ratio. A large gap indicates the RSSM's predictions degrade substantially at $\\Delta t{>}1$.\n93\t\n94\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n95\t\\section{Experiments}\n96\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{Walker Walk: Baseline Calibration}\n99\t\\label{sec:walker-baseline}\n100\t\n101\tThree seeds of the $\\Delta t{=}1$ baseline establish the reference for nve computation. All seeds converge normally to 900--960 eval return.\n102\t\n103\t\\begin{table}[h]\n104\t\\centering\n105\t\\caption{Walker Walk baseline ($\\Delta t{=}1$) across 3 seeds. v/e denotes value/eval ratio.}\n106\t\\label{tab:walker-baseline}\n107\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n108\t\\toprule\n109\tSeed & eval@105K & v/e@35K & v/e@105K & v/e stable range \\\\\n110\t\\midrule\n111\tseed 0 (001) & 916 & 0.241 & 0.656 & 69--72\\% \\\\\n112\tseed 1 (009d) & 908 & 0.215 & 0.635 & 67--71\\% \\\\\n113\tseed 2 (009a) & 919 & 0.248 & 0.699 & 69--71\\% \\\\\n114\t\\midrule\n115\tIQM $\\pm$ CI & $916 \\pm 14$ & $0.235 \\pm 0.017$ & $0.663 \\pm 0.033$ & --- \\\\\n116\t\\bottomrule\n117\t\\end{tabular}\n118\t\\end{table}\n119\t\n120\tThe 15\\% cross-seed variance in v/e@35K ($[0.215, 0.248]$) means the 1.5$\\times$ nve threshold corresponds to different absolute v/e levels across seeds, introducing noise into threshold-based classification.\n121\t\n122\t\\subsection{Walker Walk: Trained $\\Delta t$-Embedding (Delusional Critic)}\n123\t\\label{sec:walker-trained}\n124\t\n125\tWith trained $\\Delta t$-embedding and temporal subsampling (config 008d), we observe the delusional critic mechanism---but only in 1 of 3 seeds.\n126\t\n127\t\\begin{table}[h]\n128\t\\centering\n129\t\\caption{Walker Walk, trained $\\Delta t$-embedding (008d config), nve trajectory across 3 seeds. Values marked * at 25K are unreliable (baseline v/e = 0.087).}\n130\t\\label{tab:walker-008d}\n131\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n132\t\\toprule\n133\tStep & seed 0 (008d) nve & seed 1 (009e) nve & seed 2 (009b) nve \\\\\n134\t\\midrule\n135\t25K & 1.78* & 1.45* & 2.05* \\\\\n136\t35K & 1.71 & 1.35 & 1.24 \\\\\n137\t45K & 2.09 & 1.28 & 1.62 \\\\\n138\t55K & $4.12 \\rightarrow$ collapse & 1.15 & 1.41 \\\\\n139\t105K & collapsed & 0.82 & 0.38 \\\\\n140\t\\bottomrule\n141\t\\end{tabular}\n142\t\\end{table}\n143\t\n144\tSeed 0 exhibits sustained nve $>$ 1.5$\\times$ across three consecutive checkpoints (1.71$\\times$, 2.09$\\times$, 4.12$\\times$), culminating in catastrophic collapse. Seed 2 transiently exceeds 1.5$\\times$ at 45K (1.62$\\times$) but self-corrects, reaching eval 268@105K without collapse. Seed 1 never exceeds 1.5$\\times$ (peak 1.35$\\times$ at 35K) and stabilizes at nve = 0.82$\\times$. Collapse probability: 1/3 (33\\%).\n145\t\n146\t\\subsection{Walker Walk: No $\\Delta t$-Embedding (Causal Control)}\n147\t\\label{sec:walker-notrain}\n148\t\n149\tRemoving the $\\Delta t$-embedding entirely ($\\texttt{dt\\_embed\\_dim}{=}0$, config 008f) serves as a causal control.\n150\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01572bQizBubcV4Kjo3jZRQ1","session_id":"aa49a6eb-652d-4e6b-ad9c-09d11a6223fd","uuid":"4c5aba6b-a19f-4de3-bc14-80b30b51b6d3","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:03:23.113Z"}
